00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
the united states has urged russia to  declare that it has no intention of  invading ukraine speaking at the u.n  security council u.s secretary of state  anthony blinken repeated warnings that  russia is poised to launch an attack  russia insisted that it is withdrawing  troops and accused ukraine of failing to  implement a previous peace deal  meanwhile there are reports of fighting  in ukraine's east with the government  and pro-russia separatists accusing each  other of carrying out shelling attacks  in this government-held village in  eastern ukraine a kindergarten hit by a  shell  the ukrainian army says the shell that  was fired from the other side hit just  into this kindergarten there where you  can see this hole in the wall and it  fell into  the place where the kids were going to  have their gym class luckily none of the  kids was inside that room  children were having breakfast at the  time and were planning to have lessons  in that room at 9 00 am according to  their schedule  luckily the children were not heard only  the employees were shell-shocked  u.s president joe biden believes a  russian invasion will take place any day  now they have not moved any of their  church out they've moved more troops in  number one number two  we have reason to believe that they are  engaged in a false flag operation to  have an excuse to go in  every indication we have is they're  prepared to go into a train attack  ukraine  at a un security council meeting u.s  secretary of state anthony blinken  warned russia was already manufacturing  a provocation to justify an attack  this could be a violent event that  russia will bring on ukraine or an  outrageous accusation that russia will  level against the ukrainian government  moscow has already accused ukraine of  committing genocide in the eastern  donbass region and labeled the false  flag claims as hysteria  nobody seeks any excuses  for hostile elections  we said we do not have any intention to  do this  for days russia has said its troops are  withdrawing from areas near the border  with ukraine but the west believes  moscow has amassed some 150 000 soldiers  around its neighbor the conflict is far  from over  dw correspondent matthias bullinger is  in eastern ukraine in the town of  kramatorsk near the front line in the  conflict between government forces and  russia-backed separatists i asked him if  the shelling of the nursery that he  pointed out in his report was a sign  that tensions in the region are growing  yes tensions are growing that's what we  can say for sure there has been intense  shelling for two days now uh after we  left that place where the kindergarten  is there the place was shelled again and  uh houses were destroyed and were on  fire at night  and the same is reported from other  places villages and towns along the  contact line  so there's a clear escalation here the  ukrainian side blames it all on the  other side on the separatist separatist  side and there is a lot of conflicting  information the uosc has not established  yet uh all of the uh details for for  these fires i have often uh just  refrained from uh clearly stating or or  have not been able to to clearly say  where it was coming from but what what  is very clear is that shelling has  intensified on this  on this side of the of the border  shelling uh on ukrainian villages we  have less information from the other  side  right so there where you are in the east  of ukraine that's a place where the  fighting never really stopped are people  there afraid of a wider russian invasion  people here are afraid of an escalation  of an intensifying of fighting here as  you said it has never stopped its there  has been fighting for eight years  although at times ceasefires  were in place and they were respected at  times they were violated only slightly  and now we are seeing this escalation  the prospect of a wider invasion that  would include many other parts of  ukraine that would for example include  an incursion from the north  onto kiev for example that is something  of course people in other parts of  ukraine are worried about however uh the  worries uh here in ukraine do not match  the international warnings the ukrainian  government has said once again that they  do not see preparations on the russian  side for a full-scale invasion  and  the people are just  watching this carefully  they are alert but they are not  panicking  and there's this dispute about whether  russia really has pulled some of its  troop back from its border with ukraine  what's your understanding of what's  going on  well it seems that this has not really  happened when they announced the  withdrawal of troops there was some  activity troops were being moved around  but  the first thing is that russia said it  would move back  to the troops from the western  military district that is those places  that are anyway close to the ukrainian  borders back into their  into their barracks but they could be  deployed quickly again now what we are  hearing from intelligence sources is  that they have not really withdrawn many  they have moved them a little bit around  but not withdrawn some are even saying  the u.s have even said that they might  have reinforced them or this is based on  satellite imagery uh there is no  headcount of course of soldiers  no headcount accessible uh to anybody  else but the russian leadership of the  soldiers that are really deployed there  okay thank you for that matthias dw  correspondent matthias bullinger in  eastern ukraine  let's talk to ian lesser who's vice  president of the american public policy  think tank the german marshall fund  we'll talk about the the the problems on  ukraine's border he joins us from  brussels welcome to dw um people are  fond of saying that diplomacy is working  as long as no one is shooting but it's  hard to see  what the last few weeks of intense high  level  diplomacy has achieved  well that's exactly right uh of course  the diplomacy is essential but the  dynamics change as time has gone on and  as forces have been concentrated around  ukraine as the risk of something going  seriously wrong whatever russian  intentions are uh will increase and so  uh there are two  challenges here one is crisis management  immediate crisis management which so far  does not seem to have been successful  and the other is to see if there isn't  some way uh  to  talk to russia about some of its  longer-term concerns i mean president  macron has tried to do that that too has  not yet been successful and that's a  longer-term project  so  there seems to be little trust in this  process as well when we have russia  saying that it's withdrawing troops and  the u.s saying and its allies saying  well we've checked and you really  haven't again  it's hard to see how diplomacy can work  without trust  there's very very little trust i mean  that's that's absolutely clear  what is also clear is that it's possible  to see almost everything intelligence  services even commercial satellite  operators can provide uh overhead  imagery which makes you know in other  kinds of intelligence being gathered  makes it very clear what is happening  but we're judging intentions  is much tougher and there we really do  get to this question of trust and trust  at the moment is i don't know if it's at  rock bottom but it's certainly at a  minimum on both sides  and talking about addressing russia's  security concerns that  cynically speaking if russia wants to  keep uh  ukraine out of nato then short of nato  or ukraine agreeing to this demand then  all moscow really has to do is maintain  the instability at the border  is that a sustainable policy in the long  term  well i have to think that russia  understands very well that nato that  ukraine is not becoming a member of nato  anytime soon perhaps never i mean this  is very clear it's one thing to have a  declaratory open door of  which is nato policy nato allies aren't  going to back away from that but the  politics of it are also very clear that  nato simply isn't joining uh sorry  ukraine isn't joining nato anytime soon  they may be equally concerned over the  long term of ukraine just simply coming  closer to  the european union coming closer to the  west and all that that would mean for  society and politics inside russia  itself and that may be the longer  concern the longer game that russia is  trying to play to prevent that uh you  know it's pretty clear that the the  tougher they can make the situation look  in ukraine the harder it will be for  ukraine to be  integrated into the west right and  what's your view on the way that u.s  european cooperation in this situation  has worked is this a well functioning  machine  i think it's been very very close  actually and you know there will be  there's a package of sanctions being  developed on the european side but  developed in close coordination with the  united states these haven't been  revealed in detail yet but my sense is  that on on a an hourly basis a constant  basis the eu and the us are talking to  each other about what should be in that  package and about policy towards ukraine  in general it's it's a huge shift from  the kind of atmosphere we had in u.s eu  relations just a few years ago obviously  all right good talking thank you so much  for joining us ian lesser vice president  of the german marshall fund  good to be with you  well the ukraine crisis is expected to  dominate the munich security conference  which begins today u.s vice president  kamala harris is one of the first  foreign representatives to arrive in  germany from the annual three-day policy  forum which was launched at the height  of the cold war for the first time since  1991 russia will not be attending dw  will bring you a full coverage from  munich this afternoon  uh one of the people you'll be seeing  lots of in our coverage of the security  conference is our chief political editor  michaela kufner welcome michaela let's  talk about russia no russia delegation  for the first time in 30 years why  yeah that's a big question well clearly  russia is not interested in engaging in  detail on the goings-on not just over  ukraine but also over other  crisis regions there's a lot to talk  about at this munich security conference  but the current standoff which as we  heard not just anthony blinken also the  u.s president fears could tip over into  a hot war at any point  now  is clearly overshadowing every  conversation that is taking place on the  multiple other crises that really need  talking about after all this munich  security conference which is seen most  importantly as a forum where leaders can  meet in these side rooms can have  private conversations out of the  spotlight while us journalists are  talking here in the full glare um of the  public  hasn't really taken place for two years  now so it's about time that these  leaders can meet and with a great  urgency to have such conversations  russia clearly isn't interested in  talking in detail  thank you for that dw chief political  editor michaela kufner at the munich  security conference

